    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.4,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of the magnitude of offensive reorganization, following a significant player absence, on the change in a team's points per possession in subsequent games, controlling for relevant confounding factors.",
    "unit": "Player absence event, defined as a 'significant' player (e.g., top-N usage, starter) missing a game for which they were previously available. The unit of observation for 'change in points per possession' will be a team's performance in a defined post-absence period.",
    "sample": "All 'significant' player absence events from the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 NBA regular seasons and playoffs. Filter to include only absences leading to sufficient post-absence game data (e.g., >=5 games) to calculate stable PPP. Further filter based on a minimum threshold for 'reorganization' to ensure a meaningful signal.",
    "power": "Target a minimum of 100-150 valid absence events after all filtering. Power analysis will be conducted based on the expected effect size and variability of PPP changes, assuming a medium effect size (e.g., Cohen's f^2 = 0.15) for a multiple regression model. If the sample size falls below N=100 after filtering, the study will be re-evaluated for power.",
    "controls": "Control for: 1. Quality of absent player (e.g., previous season's PPP, usage, All-Star status). 2. Quality of replacement player(s) (if identifiable). 3. Opponent strength in pre/post periods (e.g., opponent's season-average defensive rating). 4. Game context (e.g., home/away, back-to-back, lead/lag in score). 5. Other concurrent player absences. 6. Team's overall season-to-date performance trend. 7. Lagged team PPP. A fixed-effects model for teams or players, or difference-in-differences approach, will be explored.",
    "falsification": "A falsification test will involve analyzing 'pseudo-absences' where a significant player is present but plays unusually low minutes, or where a 'reorganization' event is artificially detected without a player absence. If these events show a similar relationship between 'reorganization' and PPP change, it would challenge the specificity of the findings.",
    "multiplicity": "Given the single primary estimand (correlation/prediction), p-value adjustment for multiple testing is not strictly required. However, if multiple measures of 'reorganization' are explored, a Bonferroni or Holm-Bonferroni correction will be applied to the p-values for those exploratory analyses.",
